00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
chances are where you are in your  architectural career right now in 2020  i've been there and today i really want  to give you  some invaluable advice  what's going on guys my name is david  thomage and welcome back to the channel  for those of you who don't know me i'm a  masters of architecture graduate  currently undergoing my final  registration process to become a fully  registered architect  in australia today i wanted to take the  time to talk to you about your  architectural journey and what you're  about to experience  over the next five years if you're just  starting out  it is one hell of a journey it is  something that you will definitely  remember for the rest of your life  but there's a few things i wish people  told me before i got started  if you've been subscribed to the channel  for a little while now first thank you  so much  if you haven't subscribe button's just  there but you'll probably  be aware of the fact that recently i  ruptured my achilles tendon  well last time i said that i was going  to see the physio and the specialist  and yes i have gotten out of the cast  i'm in a moon boot  it was one of the most painful  experiences i've experienced in a very  long time  but progress is progress and i'm slowly  making my way  back to being able to walk and be on my  feet again so  very good news anyway enough about me  and my life story  let's get on with today's video so my  number one piece of advice  is prepare to be uncomfortable from  somebody who considers himself an  introvert  i thought architecture school was one of  the most challenging  social aspects i've ever been exposed to  and that's because my tutors and my  peers around me  really really pushed me let me give you  an example on my very first day  at architecture school it was day one  studio  number one it was the most out of the  box studio that we've ever done  but what we actually did for that studio  was nude  live model sketches yup you heard me  right  nude live model sketches was the very  first  day of architecture school not many  people say on the first day of  university  their tutors put nude people in front of  them we were split up into three  different classes because it was a  pretty big architecture group  and we each got one live subject that we  had to sketch  and try to get ourselves to understand  the human form  now there are a number of lessons in  this but the predominant one that i took  out of it after reflecting on it  many many years later was the fact that  architecture is a very  uncomfortable profession and you have to  be ready to come out of your shell  if you're somebody who is like i was a  very much an introvert  you are going to get exposed to many  many things and you are going to be  criticized  a lot i can't stress that last point  enough  architecture is such an opinionated  subject that it doesn't matter what you  design  somebody is going to think it is the  worst thing ever it's very similar to  being on youtube actually  you might put out the best video ever  but i guarantee you there's going to be  a number of people  that comment worst thing you've ever put  out but hey  everybody's entitled to their own  opinion it's just something that we have  to get comfortable with  as people in the profession number two  if you think you need  the skills and ability to draw like an  artist well  you've definitely got that wrong  architecture is no longer about your  skills on drawing on paper  yes there are still some elements and  some schools that do teach  the manual arts and the technical  drawing which is fantastic because it's  a great skill to have  but you don't have to be technically  perfect to be  a good architect or even a good  architecture student in my personal  opinion  now in 2020 the most important thing  that you're going to learn in  architecture school  is the technology side of things  architecture has progressed so much  that all and i mean literally all of  our documentation is done on a computer  from sketch design  all the way through to final  construction documentation you do not  need to pick up a pencil  ever in your architecture career to be a  good  architect again there is some value in  it you can sketch in front of clients  for example  and try to figure out problems in front  of builders  but overall as a general skill it isn't  the number one on your list  that you must know how to draw i think  what is more important  is one understanding technology and  understanding  how to use these programs which  obviously come through practice and  repetition  same way as drawing did back in the day  i think number two what you'll find is a  lot of architecture students get into  photography and videography  very early on now this is because it  lets you express your creative freedom  and understand  the building as a whole which we'll get  to a little bit later because the  building  isn't actually what makes good  architecture i think if you're willing  to venture again  out of your comfort zone and learn  technology learn some photography and  videography skills  you're gonna do exceptionally well at  architecture school  okay so i just said architecture isn't  about buildings  which brings me to point number three so  point number three you have to be  really really good at hitting that like  button and smashing that subscribe  button  no i'm just kidding but i would really  appreciate it if you hit that like  button for me  architecture is about yes the building  itself  but it's more so about the building and  how the building functions  how the spaces work how the flow works  who it's designed for  and why it has been designed there is  absolutely no point  designing a one million person stadium  in a town that only has a hundred  thousand people  yes they may have the budget the  capabilities the facilities whatever  but you need to know what this building  is  specifically being designed for so my  first year of architecture school i  couldn't grasp this concept  i always started drawing some cool  little abstract buildings and i was like  yeah this is gonna do really really well  and well i got average grades in my  first year i'm not gonna lie it wasn't  the best  until i got some really good tutors and  some really insightful people  that sat me down and went david this  isn't  architecture architecture isn't just  about how pretty the building is  you really have to walk through it  experience it  understand it and create a journey out  of that whole experience  now that word journey actually defined  my thesis project  but more so to the fact was that i  started to understand  how this was related to architecture and  the building itself so a very famous  example is le corbusier's human figure  it's the guy standing up like this i'm  sure you've seen it already if you  haven't you will see it probably  very early on into your architecture  days this little figure is  absolutely critical and pivotal to any  design that you do in the early days  i'd suggest cutting out this little guy  and placing him  into every single little abstract design  that you do  to understand that space you have to  stop designing  in a 2d floor plan point of view  and actually start imagining this space  in 3d  and in section so the le caboosia figure  for example  off the top of my head i believe stands  at 2250  which means 2250 is your more  comfortable space for most of humanity  live in  if you can't get these spaces or you're  trying to create something different  then that's a whole other story but  again it brings it back to the point  that these spaces  are designed for the people that  interact with it they're designed  from the floor plan itself to the height  of the walls to the width of the room  to absolutely everything how you flow  through the building  is also critical and then when you get  really into the fine details it becomes  about materiality and texture  understanding that a transition in  materiality  could signify something very important  in the building  if it's a museum it could signify from  one decade  to another or one century to another  there is  many many ways of transitioning between  spaces  on a more i guess emotional level than a  very physical level  so stop thinking that architecture is  just about buildings  yes it is but it's more about the people  the spaces and the places  number four if you're not a people  person again get comfortable  you're going to be working in teams a  lot throughout your architecture career  there is nobody that i know in the world  that can do  everything themselves from the very  first day to the very last day  working in teams in an architecture  environment is  the general day-to-day norm so you're  going to have to find  some very good friends at the start who  you're going to be able to team up with  later on during your degrees these  people will probably end up being some  of the best friends you have for the  rest of your life  you'll create some incredible memories  and also some incredible architecture  along the way  yes you all have your own diverse style  of architecture  and you'll all eventually be very  different architects one day  but at the end of the day you would have  shared those experiences together  gone through those hard yards together  and made those memories  together now my last tip is something  that's a bit unique to architecture  if you're an architecture student sleep  is the most  vital thing that you can get but it's  also  the biggest unicorn in architecture  school i don't know why it is and i  don't know why  architecture students can't control  their time management  because i was very good at controlling  time management  during university i ended up running  multiple businesses working full-time  running my degree full time and  completing everything successfully  so i knew time management very very well  but at the same time  i spent 48 hours once wide awake  non-stop at my laptop doing this  final project for submission i was  miles ahead in my head before i started  but i think the one thing that you find  in architecture school is  there's always something that can be  improved so you'll finish a floor plan  and then you'll improve it  and then you'll finish an elevation and  then you'll improve it and you'll finish  the section and then you'll improve it  and you'll just keep improving that  project  until the very last minute that it has  to be submitted  because you really become passionate  about these projects  they become everything for you you're  consumed by them  it's three to six months of your life of  analyzing  this project and understanding every  detail about it  yours specifically and then presenting  your heart and soul to people who are  probably going to rip it apart  but that's a good thing don't feel  discouraged when they do rip it apart  because that  is a pivotal learning experience  understanding  where the faults are in these designs is  what's going to make you  a much much better architect in the  future so whenever you can get a spare  moment to sleep  make sure you do rest and recover  because  being at university for 24 48 hours  wide awake straight is very unhealthy  and very dangerous  so if you are there for long periods of  time  find somewhere that you can sleep  because we've all done it  there's no judgment there you will end  up sleeping at the architecture building  hands down guaranteed i've fallen asleep  on chairs  in random studios in a hammock once  just i said to the guys i'm gonna go lay  down for 30 seconds  next minute it was three hours later  because i'd been awake  for 36 hours for that project i think  so yeah get the sleep and make sure you  get good nights  when you can anyway that's all from me  today guys i hope you enjoyed the video  i hope i gave you some good insight into  what architecture school is going to be  like  don't get me wrong don't be put off by  this topic it is an amazing experience  and it is some of the best years you  will have of your life  you will develop some amazing skills and  create some amazing friendships if you  enjoyed the video make sure you smash  the like button  to help this video get shared around  youtube and help it with the algorithm  hit the subscribe button if you want to  see more of these videos  and as always i will see you next monday

